From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 01:01:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 021/184] segacd: verify the PCM driver against MAME; two more
 bugs out of it

Checked every field against MAME's rf5c68.cpp rather than leaving it
inferred from megadev's usage. The register semantics all hold: CTRL
bit 7 enable and bit 6 bank/channel mode, on/off inverted at 0x08, ST
shifted 8+11 so it is the high byte of a 16-bit address over 11
fractional bits, FD added straight to that counter so 0x0800 is one
sample per tick, sign-magnitude samples with bit 7 for positive, and
0xFF looping to LS and halting when LS holds 0xFF too -- the
self-terminating one-shot we already relied on.

Two real bugs fell out:

- A note cannot be retriggered by writing "on" again. The address
  counter reloads from ST only on the disable transition, so an
  already-on channel just carries on from wherever it was, which past
  the end marker is silence. Typing quickly would have produced one
  tone and then nothing, since each keypress refreshed the countdown
  that would have switched the channel off. pcm_beep now writes off
  then on.
- Channels were not silenced before the wave RAM load; megadev's
  pcm_clear_ram disables all eight first.

The emulated channel address now advances -- 0000 017a 02f4 on both
cores -- which is the first direct evidence the chip runs at all.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 7f3e393..22fca2a 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -139,6 +139,7 @@ static void pcm_load_wave(void)
 {
     UWORD i;
 
+    pcm_reg(PCM_ONOFF, 0xFF);           /* silence every channel first: */
     pcm_reg(PCM_CTRL, 0x80);            /* enable, wave RAM bank 0 */
     for (i = 0; i < PCM_LEN; i++) {
         UWORD ph = (UWORD)(i % PCM_CYCLE);
@@ -176,11 +177,19 @@ void segacd_sound_init(void)
     segacd_bell();
 }
 
+/* A channel's address counter is reloaded from ST only when the channel
+ * is switched *off*, never when it is switched on. So a note has to be
+ * retriggered off-then-on: writing "on" to a channel that is already on
+ * does nothing, and it would carry on from wherever it had got to --
+ * which, once it has reached the end marker, is silence. Without this,
+ * typing quickly gave one tone and then nothing, because each keypress
+ * refreshed the countdown that would have switched the channel off. */
 static void pcm_beep(UBYTE frames, UBYTE volume)
 {
     pcm_reg(PCM_CTRL, 0xC0);            /* channel 0 registers */
     pcm_reg(PCM_ENV, volume);
-    pcm_reg(PCM_ONOFF, 0xFE);           /* channel 0 on, the rest off */
+    pcm_reg(PCM_ONOFF, 0xFF);           /* off: rewinds it to ST */
+    pcm_reg(PCM_ONOFF, 0xFE);           /* and on again from the start */
     pcm_countdown = frames;
 }
 
